<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-exponentiation-of-natural-numbers" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Exponentiation of natural numbers
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Let <m> e \colon \N^{ 2 } \to \N </m> be the <term> exponentiation </term> operation defined by the formula
      
      <md>
        e \of{ m , n } = m^{ n }
      </md>;
      
      that is, <m> e </m> is defined recursively by the formulae <m> e \of{ m , 0 } = 1 </m> and <m> e \of{ m , n + 1 } = e^{ m , n } \cdot m </m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute <m> e \of{ 1 , 2 } </m> and <m> e \of{ 2 , 1 } </m>. Is exponentiation a commutative binary operation?
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m> e \of{ 1 , 2 } = 1^{ 2 } = 1 </m> and <m> e \of{ 2 , 1 } = 2^{ 1 } = 2 </m>. Since <m> e \of{ 1 , 2 } \neq e \of{ 2 , 1 } </m>, exponentiation is non-commutative.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute <m> e \of{ e \of{ 2 , 1 } , 2 } </m> and <m> e \of{ 2 , e \of{ 1 , 2 } } </m>. Is exponentiation an associative binary operation?
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        We compute
        
        <md>
          e \of{ e \of{ 2 , 1 } , 2 } = e \of{ 2^{ 1 } , 2 } = e \of{ 2 , 2 } = 2^{ 2 } = 4
        </md>
        
        and
        
        <md>
          e \of{ 2 , e \of{ 1 , 2 } } = e \of{ 2 , 1^{ 2 } } = e \of{ 2 , 1 } = 2^{ 1 } = 2
        </md>.
        
        Since <m> e \of{ e \of{ 2 , 1 } , 2 } \neq e \of{ 2 , e \of{ 1 , 2 } } </m>, exponentiation is non-associative.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task>
</exercise>
